<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ziaur Rahman</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1936">1936</a>–<a href="/years/1981">1981</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ziaur Rahman was a Bangladeshi army officer who announced the declaration of independence on radio in March 1971 and commanded forces in the Liberation War. After the coups of 1975 he became the country's leading figure and was president from 1977. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a> Nationalist Party and was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> in 1981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziaur-rahman.jpg" alt="Ziaur Rahman" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kbsBD · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangladeshi_president_Ziaur_Rahman,_Bir_Uttom.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Ziaur Rahman was born on 19 January 1936 in the village of Bagbari in Gabtali, Bogura District. He graduated from the Pakistan Military Academy in September 1955 and was commissioned in the Pakistan Army, serving in the Punjab Regiment and from 1957 in the East Bengal Regiment. In June 1969 he was posted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> as second-in-command of the 8th East Bengal Regiment.</p><p>After the army crackdown and the arrest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sheikh-mujibur-rahman">Sheikh Mujibur Rahman</a> on 26 March 1971, he revolted, and on 27 March he broadcast the declaration of independence from the Kalurghat radio station, becoming known as the Announcer of the Liberation. He commanded Sector 1 of the Bangladesh Forces, then Sector 11 from June and Z Force from mid-July.</p><p>After Mujibur Rahman was assassinated on 15 August 1975, Ziaur Rahman became Chief of Army Staff. A coup on 3 November forced him out and put him under house arrest, and on 7 November the Sipahi-Janata Revolution freed him and returned him to the post. He then held de facto power under martial law, took over the presidency on 21 April 1977 and retired from the army in 1978 as a lieutenant general.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Ziaur Rahman lifted martial law and in 1978 won a five-year term as president; elections to the National Assembly followed the next year, and opponents questioned their integrity. In 1978 he founded the Bangladesh Nationalist Party and reinstated multi-party politics and freedom of the press. He announced a 19-point programme stressing self-reliance, rural development, decentralisation, free markets and population control, set up village councils called Gram Sarkar, and launched a food-for-work programme in 1977.</p><p>He improved relations with the West and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and moved away from Mujib's close alignment with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. He also amended the constitution to open its preamble with an invocation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> and to replace the commitment to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secularism">secularism</a> with trust and faith in Almighty Allah. Critics say this changed the nature of the republic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Ziaur Rahman faced many coup attempts, for which military tribunals were set up and at least 200 soldiers of the army and air force were executed. On 29 May 1981 he travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a> to settle a dispute in the regional BNP and stayed at the Circuit House. In the early hours of 30 May a group of army officers assassinated him; six bodyguards and two aides were also killed. Nearly two million people are estimated to have attended his funeral. Opinion on his legacy is divided: he is credited with ending disorder and restoring multi-party politics, while critics say he suppressed opposition and gave posts to men accused in the killing of Mujibur Rahman.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ziaur Rahman” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1979 Prime Minister Shah Azizur Rahman proposed Shahnaz Rahmatullah's &quot;Prothom Bangladesh&quot; instead, and under President <b>Ziaur Rahman</b> that song was played after the anthem on national television and at government events until his death in 1981.</blockquote><a href="/songs/amar-sonar-bangla">Amar Sonar Bangla</a></li><li><blockquote>Sheikh Mujib led the new state until his assassination in 1975; military rulers <b>Ziaur Rahman</b> and Hussain Muhammad Ershad followed.</blockquote><a href="/state/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Bogura District, in what is now <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chittagong">Chittagong</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/bangladesh">Bangladesh</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=24.78,89.35&amp;year=1965" aria-label="Bogura District on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6922 1252 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ziaur Rahman"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7372 1522h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7372 1522h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=24.78,89.35&amp;year=1965">What was here in 1965? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
